BODY = """
<h3>A precursor state with uneven risk</h3>
<p>The modern view of smoldering myeloma starts with the Mayo cohort of 276 patients: progression to myeloma or amyloidosis occurred at about 10% per year for the first 5 years, about 3% per year for the next 5 and about 1% per year thereafter, reaching 73% at 15 years [[Mayo natural history (Kyle)]]. Two biomarkers soon refined that picture: flow cytometry, where a high proportion of aberrant plasma cells with immunoparesis separated 5-year progression risks of 4%, 46% and 72% [[PETHEMA flow-cytometry risk (Pérez-Persona)]], and the serum free light chain ratio [[Free light chain ratio (Dispenzieri)]].</p>
<p>In 2014, the IMWG moved patients with any of three biomarkers of near-certain progression (60% or more clonal plasma cells, a light chain ratio of 100 or more, or more than one focal MRI lesion) into active myeloma [[IMWG updated criteria (Rajkumar)]]. That decision matters for interpreting every trial: patients called "smoldering" before 2014 included some who would now be treated as having myeloma. Population screening in Iceland later showed that smoldering myeloma is present in about 0.5% of people aged 40 or older, mostly with low-risk features [[iStopMM screening (Thorsteinsdóttir)]], a reminder that over-treatment is a real risk.</p>

<h3>Defining high risk</h3>
<p>Two simple models now dominate. The Mayo 20/2/20 model (marrow plasma cells over 20%, M-protein over 2 g/dL, light chain ratio over 20) separated median times to progression of 110, 68 and 29 months [[Mayo 20/2/20 model (Lakshman)]], and the IMWG validated the same factors in nearly 2,000 patients, with 2-year progression of 6%, 18% and 44% for low, intermediate and high risk [[IMWG 2/20/20 risk model (Mateos)]]. Because these models use a single time point, dynamic approaches that follow changes in M-protein, light chains, creatinine and hemoglobin are being developed; the PANGEA model outperformed the static models (C-statistic 0.79) [[PANGEA-SMM dynamic risk (Chabrun)]].</p>

<h3>Early treatment: from feasibility to approval</h3>
<p>Early attempts with thalidomide showed that treatment was feasible but poorly tolerated [[Thalidomide + pamidronate (Barlogie)]] [[Thalidomide + zoledronic acid (Witzig)]]. The field changed with QuiRedex, a small Spanish trial of 119 high-risk patients: lenalidomide-dexamethasone delayed progression and improved 3-year survival (94% vs 80%), with the survival benefit maintained at long-term follow-up [[QuiRedex (Mateos)]]. Its definitions predated 2014, so some patients would now be considered to have myeloma. ECOG E3A06 confirmed that lenalidomide alone delays progression (3-year PFS 91% vs 66%), mainly in high-risk patients, without a survival difference [[ECOG E3A06 (Lonial)]]. Not every agent worked: IL-6 blockade with siltuximab did not meet its endpoint [[Siltuximab (Brighton)]].</p>
<p>Daratumumab provided the decisive trial. After CENTAURUS selected a prolonged dosing schedule [[CENTAURUS (Landgren)]], AQUILA randomized 390 high-risk patients to subcutaneous daratumumab for up to 3 years or active monitoring: 5-year PFS was 63.1% vs 40.8% (HR 0.49) and 5-year overall survival 93.0% vs 86.9% (HR 0.52), with few discontinuations for toxicity [[AQUILA (Dimopoulos)]]. Approval followed in the EU in July 2025 and in the US on November 6, 2025, the first treatment approved for smoldering myeloma.</p>

<h3>Curative intent and immunotherapy</h3>
<p>A parallel line of studies asks whether high-risk smoldering myeloma can be cured rather than delayed. Carfilzomib-lenalidomide-dexamethasone produced MRD-negative complete responses in 70% [[NCI KRd (Korde, Kazandjian)]]; the transplant-based GEM-CESAR approach reached undetectable MRD in 62% after transplant, with 70-month overall survival of 92% [[GEM-CESAR (Mateos)]]; and ixazomib-lenalidomide-dexamethasone linked MRD negativity to durable control [[I-PRISM (Nadeem)]]. Most recently, fixed-duration teclistamab produced complete responses in 77.8% and a 2-year PFS of 92% vs 49% with lenalidomide-dexamethasone in a small randomized phase 2 trial [[ImmunoPRISM (Nadeem)]]. These remain trial approaches.</p>

<h3>Where we are now</h3>
<p>The first steps are to confirm that the patient truly has smoldering myeloma (no SLiM-CRAB features, with appropriate imaging) and to estimate risk. For low- and intermediate-risk disease, observation remains standard. For high-risk disease, guidance differs. NCCN (January 2026 version, as summarized on this page; not checked against the full document) recommends treatment with careful selection and shared decision-making, including daratumumab, and lists lenalidomide as an option. The 2026 ASCO-Ontario living guideline says daratumumab may be offered, does not routinely recommend lenalidomide and advises no therapy for non-high-risk disease [[ASCO-Ontario Health living guideline (Hicks)]]. A 2026 Cochrane review grades the evidence for early intervention as low certainty [[Cochrane review: early intervention (Chen)]], and the 2021 European consensus, written before AQUILA, favored observation outside trials [[EMN consensus statement (Musto)]]. In practice, the decision is individualized.</p>

<h3>Open questions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Delay or cure:</b> is the goal to postpone myeloma with low-toxicity therapy or to attempt eradication in the highest-risk patients?</li>
<li><b>Who is high risk:</b> static versus dynamic models, and the role of cytogenetics and imaging.</li>
<li><b>Over-treatment:</b> screening finds many low-risk patients who may never need therapy.</li>
<li><b>Later consequences:</b> how early anti-CD38 or T-cell-directed therapy affects responses at progression, and infection risk in asymptomatic people.</li>
<li><b>Survival:</b> only QuiRedex and AQUILA report survival gains, and both have limitations.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What is coming</h3>
<p>Ongoing phase 3 trials (listed under Trials to watch at the end of this page) compare daratumumab-lenalidomide-dexamethasone with lenalidomide-dexamethasone with overall survival as the endpoint (DETER-SMM), isatuximab-lenalidomide-dexamethasone with lenalidomide-dexamethasone, and linvoseltamab with daratumumab (LINKER-SMM2).</p>
"""
